{"id":"h233","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"A researcher notices that the frequency of a note emitted by an automobile horn appears to drop from 284 cycles \\bullet s^-1 to 266 cycles \\bullet s^-1 as the automobile passes him. From this observation he is able to calculate the speed of the car, knowing that the speed of sound in air is 1100 ft \\bullet s^-1. What value does he obtain for the speed?\nIs the correct answer \"24.5 mph\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The horn is a moving source and the researcher is stationary, so the moving-source Doppler formulas apply.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Approaching frequency is f₀·v/(v−u) and receding frequency is f₀·v/(v+u).","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Taking the ratio of frequencies eliminates the unknown emitted frequency f₀, giving (v+u)/(v−u)=284/266.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Solving gives the car's speed u = 36 ft/s.","confidence":0.93,"lean":1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":0.0},{"text":"36 ft/s converts to about 24.5 mph.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Treating the horn as stationary and the observer as moving, or using only one frequency, would give an undetermined or wrong result.","confidence":0.6,"lean":0.0,"theta":0.9425,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The problem wording supports the moving-source interpretation.","confidence":0.8,"lean":1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Rounding of the given frequencies could shift the last digit but the result is 24.5 mph to the given precision.","confidence":0.7,"lean":1,"theta":1.0996,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The answer 24.5 mph is correct.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"